    body: `<p>The existence of denominations is an embarrassment to many Christians and a stumbling block to many outsiders. "Why can't they just agree?" is the perennial question, and it is not a foolish one. But the assumption behind it — that the differences between denominations are petty or trivial — is almost always wrong. The major denominations exist because they represent genuinely different answers to fundamental questions: What is the church? How does God save? Who has authority to interpret Scripture? How should Christians worship? What is the relationship between faith and works, between individual conviction and communal tradition, between the sovereignty of God and the freedom of the human will?</p>

<p>These are not small questions, and the denominations that have formed around different answers to them are not arbitrary. Each one carries a theological insight that the others need, and each one has blind spots that the others can correct. What follows is a guide to the major Protestant and Protestant-adjacent denominations — not a ranking, not a recommendation, but a map of the landscape, drawn with as much fairness as a single author can manage.</p>

<h2>Baptist</h2>

<p>The Baptist tradition is the largest Protestant family in America and one of the most consequential in global Christianity, with roughly 100 million adherents worldwide. Its origins lie in the early seventeenth century, among English Separatists who concluded that the Church of England's practice of baptizing infants was without scriptural warrant. The first identifiable Baptist congregation was established in Amsterdam in 1609 by John Smyth, a former Anglican clergyman, and Thomas Helwys, who returned to England to establish the first Baptist church on English soil.</p>

<p>The core Baptist distinctive is <em>believer's baptism</em>: the conviction that baptism is properly administered only to those who have made a conscious profession of faith in Christ. This is not merely a disagreement about the proper subjects of baptism; it reflects a deeper ecclesiology. For Baptists, the church is a <em>gathered</em> community of regenerate believers, not a parish encompassing all the baptized in a geographical area. Membership is voluntary, conscious, and covenantal.</p>

<p>Closely related is the principle of <em>soul competency</em> — the belief that every individual is competent before God to make their own religious decisions without the mediation of priest, creed, or state. This principle has historically made Baptists fierce advocates of religious liberty. It was a Baptist minister, John Leland, who lobbied James Madison for the inclusion of religious freedom in the Bill of Rights. Roger Williams, the founder of Rhode Island and an early Baptist (briefly), established the first government in the Western world to guarantee absolute freedom of conscience.</p>

<p>Baptist polity is congregational: each local church is autonomous, governing its own affairs without hierarchical oversight. This autonomy is both a strength and a vulnerability. It means that no denominational body can dictate theology to a local congregation. It also means that there is no mechanism for holding a local church accountable when it goes off the rails.</p>

<p>The Southern Baptist Convention (SBC), with roughly 13 million members, is the largest Baptist body in America and the largest Protestant denomination in the country. It was founded in 1845 in a split with Northern Baptists over the question of whether slaveholders could serve as missionaries — a founding sin that the SBC formally acknowledged and repented of in 1995. The SBC today is theologically conservative, holding to biblical inerrancy, complementarian gender roles, and traditional sexual ethics. The American Baptist Churches USA (ABCUSA), the successor to the Northern Baptists, is considerably more moderate, allowing local church autonomy on contested issues.</p>

<h2>Methodist</h2>

<p>Methodism originated not as a separate denomination but as a renewal movement within the Church of England, founded by John Wesley (1703-1791), one of the most remarkable figures in the history of Christianity. Wesley was an Oxford-educated Anglican clergyman who, on May 24, 1738, attended a Moravian meeting on Aldersgate Street in London and felt his heart "strangely warmed" — an experience of assurance that would become the template for Methodist conversion narratives for the next three centuries.</p>

<p>Wesley's theological contribution was the synthesis of several streams that had not previously been combined. From the Anglican tradition, he took liturgical worship, sacramental theology, and a high view of the church. From the Puritans, he took an emphasis on personal holiness and disciplined spiritual practice. From the Moravians, he took the centrality of conversion experience and assurance of salvation. From the Eastern church fathers — whom he read avidly — he took the concept of <em>theosis</em>, which he translated into the Methodist doctrine of <em>entire sanctification</em>: the belief that the Holy Spirit can so transform a believer's heart that they are freed from the power, though not the presence, of sin in this life.</p>

<p>The Wesleyan Quadrilateral — the idea that theological reflection draws on four sources: Scripture, tradition, reason, and experience — has become one of the most widely used frameworks in Protestant theology, though Wesley himself never used the term (it was coined by the twentieth-century Methodist theologian Albert Outler). Scripture holds primacy, but tradition, reason, and experience function as essential interpretive lenses.</p>

<p>The United Methodist Church, formed in 1968 through the merger of the Methodist Church and the Evangelical United Brethren, was for decades the second-largest Protestant denomination in America. In 2023-2024, it underwent a historic split over the question of same-sex marriage and the ordination of openly gay clergy. The Global Methodist Church was formed as a theologically conservative alternative, and roughly a quarter of UMC congregations disaffiliated. The split mirrored a pattern seen across mainline Protestantism: progressive and traditionalist factions, unable to reach consensus on sexuality, choosing separation over continued institutional conflict.</p>

<h2>Presbyterian</h2>

<p>Presbyterianism is the institutional expression of Reformed theology — the theological tradition that flows from John Calvin's work in sixteenth-century Geneva. Calvin was a French lawyer and theologian whose <em>Institutes of the Christian Religion</em>, first published in 1536 and expanded through multiple editions, became the most comprehensive and influential systematic theology of the Reformation. Where Luther's theology was driven by the pastoral question "How can I find a gracious God?", Calvin's was driven by the theological question "How can God be glorified in all things?"</p>

<p>The distinctives of Presbyterian theology are the distinctives of Reformed theology generally: the absolute sovereignty of God over all creation, including the salvation of individual souls; the total depravity of human nature after the Fall; unconditional election (God's choice of individuals for salvation, not based on foreseen merit); the efficacy of Christ's atoning death for the elect; the irresistibility of God's grace in calling the elect to faith; and the perseverance of the saints (those whom God has chosen will not finally fall away). These five points, codified at the Synod of Dort in 1618-1619, are popularly known by the acronym TULIP.</p>

<p>Presbyterian governance is its most visible structural distinctive. The word "Presbyterian" comes from the Greek <em>presbyteros</em> (elder), and the system is built on a hierarchy of representative courts: the session (governing body of the local church, composed of elected elders), the presbytery (a regional body of churches), the synod, and the general assembly. This system of graded courts was designed to prevent both the tyranny of bishops (which the Reformers associated with Catholicism) and the chaos of pure congregationalism. It is, in essence, a republican form of church government — representative, constitutional, and accountable.</p>

<p>The Presbyterian Church in America (PCA), founded in 1973, holds to biblical inerrancy, complementarian theology, and traditional sexual ethics. The Presbyterian Church (USA), the larger and older body, has moved in a progressive direction, ordaining women since 1956 and openly gay clergy since 2011. The two bodies represent, in miniature, the broader division within American Protestantism between conservative and progressive impulses.</p>

<h2>Lutheran</h2>

<p>Lutheranism is the oldest Protestant tradition, tracing its origin to Martin Luther's posting of the Ninety-Five Theses in Wittenberg on October 31, 1517 — though Luther himself neither intended to start a new church nor approved of the use of his name for the movement that resulted. Luther's central insight was the doctrine of justification by grace through faith: the conviction that sinful human beings are declared righteous before God not on the basis of their works, their sacramental participation, or their moral improvement, but solely on the basis of Christ's righteousness, received through faith.</p>

<p>Lutheran theology is structured around the distinction between Law and Gospel — two words of God that must never be confused. The Law reveals God's demands, exposes human sin, and drives the sinner to despair. The Gospel reveals God's grace, announces the forgiveness of sins, and creates faith. The Law tells you what you ought to do. The Gospel tells you what God has done. Every passage of Scripture, in Lutheran hermeneutics, is to be read through this lens, and the failure to distinguish Law from Gospel is the root of all theological error.</p>

<p>Luther's doctrine of the Two Kingdoms — the teaching that God governs the world through two distinct realms, the spiritual (through the church and the Gospel) and the temporal (through government and the law) — has profoundly shaped Lutheran political theology. The two realms are not opposed, but they operate by different logics and must not be confused. The church should not attempt to govern society by the Gospel, and the state should not attempt to coerce faith by the sword. This doctrine has been both a source of Lutheran political modesty (a reluctance to identify the church's mission with any political program) and a source of Lutheran political failure (a tendency to defer too readily to state authority, most catastrophically in the German churches' acquiescence to Nazism).</p>

<p>Lutheran worship retains more of the Catholic liturgical tradition than most other Protestant traditions. Luther was conservative in his liturgical reforms, retaining the basic structure of the Mass, vestments, the church year calendar, and much of the medieval hymnody. Lutheran churches typically celebrate the Eucharist weekly or biweekly and practice infant baptism, understanding both sacraments as means of grace through which God acts objectively in the lives of believers.</p>

<p>The Evangelical Lutheran Church in America (ELCA), formed in 1988, is the largest Lutheran body in the United States. It ordains women and openly gay clergy, and it entered into full communion with the Episcopal Church in 1999. The Lutheran Church-Missouri Synod (LCMS) is theologically conservative, holding to biblical inerrancy, opposing the ordination of women, and practicing closed communion (restricting the Eucharist to those who share its doctrinal confession). The Wisconsin Evangelical Lutheran Synod (WELS) is more conservative still. The distance between the ELCA and the LCMS on almost every major issue — biblical authority, sexuality, ecumenism, worship — is vast, despite their shared confessional heritage.</p>

<h2>Anglican and Episcopal</h2>

<p>Anglicanism originated in the English Reformation of the sixteenth century, though its roots stretch deeper into the distinctive Christianity of the British Isles. Henry VIII's break with Rome in 1534 was politically motivated — the pope's refusal to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon — but the theological development of Anglicanism under Thomas Cranmer, Edward VI, and Elizabeth I produced something genuinely distinctive: a church that understood itself as both Catholic and Reformed, occupying a <em>via media</em> (middle way) between Rome and Geneva.</p>

<p>The Book of Common Prayer, first published by Thomas Cranmer in 1549 and revised in 1552 and 1662, is the liturgical foundation of Anglican identity. Cranmer was a prose stylist of extraordinary gifts, and the Prayer Book's language — "We have erred and strayed from thy ways like lost sheep"; "We are not worthy so much as to gather up the crumbs under thy table" — has shaped English-speaking spirituality for nearly five centuries. Anglican theology is, in a real sense, embedded in its worship. The principle <em>lex orandi, lex credendi</em> (the law of prayer is the law of belief) operates more powerfully in Anglicanism than in almost any other tradition.</p>

<p>The "broad church" character of Anglicanism — its capacity to hold together Anglo-Catholics (who emphasize the Catholic heritage), evangelicals (who emphasize Reformed theology and biblical authority), and liberals (who emphasize reason, experience, and social justice) within a single institutional framework — has been both its greatest strength and its greatest source of internal conflict. The current Anglican Communion, comprising roughly 85 million members in 165 countries, is under severe strain over questions of sexuality, with African and Asian provinces generally holding traditional positions and Western provinces (particularly the Episcopal Church in the United States and the Anglican Church of Canada) embracing progressive ones.</p>

<p>The Episcopal Church, the American branch of the Anglican Communion, has approximately 1.6 million members. It ordained its first female priests in 1974, its first female bishop in 1989, and its first openly gay bishop (Gene Robinson, in New Hampshire) in 2003. Its liturgical tradition, intellectual culture, and social engagement continue to exercise an influence disproportionate to its declining membership numbers.</p>

<h2>Pentecostal and Charismatic</h2>

<p>Pentecostalism is, by any statistical measure, the most successful Christian movement of the twentieth century. From a single revival at the Azusa Street Mission in Los Angeles in 1906, it has grown to encompass roughly 644 million adherents worldwide — more than a quarter of all Christians on earth. Its growth has been most explosive in the Global South: sub-Saharan Africa, Latin America, and East Asia.</p>

<p>The theological center of Pentecostalism is the doctrine of <em>baptism in the Holy Spirit</em> — a post-conversion experience of divine empowerment, typically evidenced by speaking in tongues (<em>glossolalia</em>). Classical Pentecostals regard this as a distinct experience from conversion, a "second blessing" that equips believers for witness and service. The biblical warrant is the account of Pentecost in Acts 2, where the disciples received the Holy Spirit and spoke in other languages.</p>

<p>The Assemblies of God, with roughly 70 million adherents worldwide, is the largest Pentecostal denomination. It is theologically Arminian (emphasizing human free will in salvation), holds to believer's baptism by immersion, and affirms the continued operation of all the spiritual gifts described in 1 Corinthians 12-14: tongues, interpretation of tongues, prophecy, healing, miracles, discernment of spirits, and others. The Church of God in Christ (COGIC), the largest African American Pentecostal denomination, has roughly 6.5 million members in the United States and is notable for its rich musical tradition, which has profoundly influenced American gospel, soul, and rock and roll.</p>

<p>Pentecostal worship is participatory, expressive, and experiential. Services may include extended periods of singing, spontaneous prayer, altar calls, speaking in tongues, prophetic utterances, and prayers for healing. The emphasis is on the immediate, experiential presence of God — not as a theological abstraction but as a felt reality in the worship service. This experiential emphasis has made Pentecostalism extraordinarily adaptable across cultures, since the experience of the Spirit transcends the linguistic and cultural barriers that limit traditions more dependent on written theology.</p>

<h2>Churches of Christ and the Restoration Movement</h2>

<p>The Restoration Movement — also called the Stone-Campbell Movement, after its founders Barton W. Stone and Alexander Campbell — is a distinctively American tradition that emerged in the early nineteenth century from the conviction that denominationalism itself was the problem. Stone, a Presbyterian revivalist in Kentucky, and Campbell, a Scots-Irish immigrant influenced by Lockean rationalism, independently concluded that the divisions of Protestantism were the result of human creeds and confessions being imposed on the simple message of the New Testament. The solution, they argued, was to abandon all denominational labels, discard all creeds, and return to the pattern of the New Testament church.</p>

<p>The movement's motto — "Where the Scriptures speak, we speak; where the Scriptures are silent, we are silent" — captures its hermeneutical principle: the New Testament provides a binding pattern for church organization, worship, and practice, and anything not authorized by that pattern is prohibited. This principle of <em>restorationism</em> led to distinctive practices: weekly observance of the Lord's Supper (because the early church "broke bread" on the first day of the week), a cappella singing (because the New Testament does not mention instruments in worship), baptism by immersion for the remission of sins, and a refusal to adopt any creed or confession other than the Bible itself.</p>

<p>The movement eventually split into three bodies: the Churches of Christ (the most conservative, holding strictly to the a cappella tradition and restorationist hermeneutic), the Christian Churches/Churches of Christ (independent, conservative but open to instrumental music), and the Christian Church (Disciples of Christ), which evolved into a mainline Protestant denomination with progressive theology. The irony that a movement dedicated to Christian unity produced three separate bodies has not been lost on its members.</p>

<h2>What the Map Reveals</h2>

<p>Several patterns emerge when the denominations are viewed side by side. First, the major divisions in American Protestantism are not primarily theological but cultural and political. The theological distance between the ELCA and the LCMS, or between the PCUSA and the PCA, or between the UMC and the Global Methodist Church, is in many cases smaller than the cultural distance. The dividing line runs through every denomination: on one side, a progressive impulse that emphasizes inclusion, justice, and adaptation to contemporary culture; on the other, a conservative impulse that emphasizes orthodoxy, fidelity to tradition, and resistance to cultural accommodation.</p>

<p>Second, every denomination's greatest strength is also the source of its greatest vulnerability. Baptist congregational autonomy produces freedom and local responsiveness, but it also produces a lack of accountability. Presbyterian governance produces order and accountability, but it also produces bureaucracy and institutional inertia. Pentecostal experiential emphasis produces vitality and growth, but it also produces vulnerability to emotional manipulation and theological shallowness. Lutheran confessional rigor produces theological depth, but it also produces insularity and resistance to change.</p>

<p>Third, the denominational landscape is not static. It is shifting rapidly, as the old mainline-evangelical binary gives way to a more complex picture in which the fastest-growing churches are often non-denominational, the most vibrant theological work is often ecumenical, and the most pressing challenges — secularization, political polarization, the decline of institutional trust — cut across all denominational lines.</p>

<p>The Christian who understands the denominational landscape is not better than the Christian who does not. But they are better equipped — to navigate their own tradition with informed loyalty, to engage other traditions with genuine respect, and to recognize that the church of Jesus Christ is larger, stranger, and more various than any single denomination can contain.</p>`
